  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Kenya Acute JR 2022</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">4 successive failed rain seasons, coupled with other shocks such as the crisis in Ukraine, have resulted in an extreme reduction in families&#x2019; ability to access food and water in the (semi) arid counties in northern Kenya due to decreased crop production and livestock conditions, increasing food commodity prices and trekking distances to water points. 4.1 million people are food insecure, including 1.1 million in IPC4. 942,000 children under 5 are acutely malnourished: 229,000 with Severe Acute Malnutrition and 713,000 with Moderate Acute Malnutrition. Wajir county is among the most affected with 468,758 people facing food insecurity &#x2013; 60% of its population. Needs in Wajir are high with relatively low humanitarian support currently provided.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC targets 4000 HHs most in risk of food insecurity due to lack of access to food and HHs most at risk of malnutrition. These include HHs in IPC4 coupled with loss of 95% of their assets (livestock), women- and child-headed HHs, HHs with People with Disabilities, HHs with chronically ill or elderly caretakers and HHs with SGBV survivors. To ensure the most vulnerable community members are reached, the project will work with existing community-based relief committees to identify target criteria to ensure community-based targeting.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The response will target 500HHs with 3 tranches of MPC though mobile money platforms. MPC will be tied with behaviour change communication on Infant and Young Child Feeding (IYCF) in line with SC&#x2019;s evidence-based cash+ approach. Following IMAM guidelines, WARDA will conduct screening of children under five and Pregnant and Lactating Women and refer them to SC who will support 12 health facilities and 30 outreach sites with nutrition commodities and essential drugs for nutrition treatment. PLWs will be counselled on IYCF and linked to existing mother to mother support groups. 
WARDA will support the county government with fuel and daily subsistence allowance to conduct water trucking. Warda will enhance hygiene conditions of the target community through procurement and distribution of hygiene kits and menstrual hygiene kits, and by providing hygiene promotion sessions. SC and WARDA will disseminate messages on protection, GBV and safeguarding during nutrition screening, hygiene promotion and MPC distribution.</narrative>
  </description>
